            <ArticleTitle>Direct Pulp Capping in Primary Molars using Mineral Trioxide Aggregate: An in Vivo Study.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>446-449</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.17796/1053-4628-41.6.5</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To do a clinical and radiographic evaluation of the effectiveness of MTA when used as a direct pulp capping material in primary molars.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Clinical and radiographic follow-up was performed on 30 primary molars with deep caries lesions in 30 patients from 3 to 9.75 years of age. Pulps exposed during cavity preparation were treated by direct pulp capping with MTA. The follow-up clinical and radiographic examinations were carried out at different time intervals: 6, 7-12, 13-18, 19-24, and &gt;24 months after treatment.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Twenty-four teeth were evaluated during the entire observation period. Positive clinical and radiographic outcomes were achieved in 19 teeth (13 teeth were saved and 6 were exfoliated). In five teeth, complications were observed only in children under 7 years old.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Based on these clinical and radiographic results, MTA was found to be successful when used as a direct pulp capping material in primary teeth.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
